{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n% Numbers in this section are generated from provenance/ledger.yaml via\n% scripts/gen_values_tex.py and cited with \\val macros, so each traces to the run and\n% code state that produced it; see provenance/README.md. (Author note, not rendered.)\n\n\\subsection{Cross-document attention beats flat concatenation}\nOn the Wikipedia HotpotQA cross-document benchmark, the link-aware \\crossdoc model attains\na cross-document negative log-likelihood of \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc}\nagainst \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} for the flat-linked baseline over the same\n$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n}$ query--target pairs, a reduction of\n$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll}$. The effect carries over to code:\non Java RepoBench cross-document completion, cross-document attention lowers NLL across all\ntraversals --- from \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll} (random packing) to\n\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} (random-walk), with\n\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.delta_nll} (bfs) and \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.delta_nll}\n(dfs) in between --- and Python RepoBench cross-doc shows a $\\Delta$nll of\n\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll} (max\\_grants=256).\n\n\\subsection{Single-document benchmarks are unaffected}\nAcross the single-document controls (HellaSwag, ARC-easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA,\nBoolQ), the four masks are statistically indistinguishable. On HellaSwag the matched\nwiki\\_merged 4-mask set scores \\val{singledoc.hellaswag.doc_causal} (\\doccausal),\n\\val{singledoc.hellaswag.cross_doc_link} (\\crossdoc),\n\\val{singledoc.hellaswag.doc_concatenated} (\\concat), and\n\\val{singledoc.hellaswag.doc_concat_link} (\\concatlink), with overlapping 95\\% confidence\nintervals (e.g.\\ \\val{singledoc.hellaswag.doc_causal.ci} for \\doccausal vs\n\\val{singledoc.hellaswag.cross_doc_link.ci} for \\crossdoc) --- cross-document attention has\nnothing to attend to on isolated documents, as predicted. These models are undertrained\n(accuracies near chance), so the claim is that the mask does not \\emph{degrade}\nsingle-document capability, not a strong-capability equivalence.\n\n\\subsection{Compute controls}\nGranting the baselines more within-context attention does not close the gap. On code\nRepoBench perplexity, \\crossdoc reaches \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.cross_doc_link}, whereas\n\\concatlink and \\concat --- which see the same concatenated multi-document context --- sit at\n\\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concat_link} and \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_concatenated}\nrespectively, and the plain \\doccausal baseline at \\val{compute.repobench_ppl.doc_causal}.\nThe compute controls remain well above \\crossdoc, so the gain comes from the linking\ninductive bias, not from additional attention FLOPs.\n\n\\subsection{Traversal ablation}\nGraph-structured packing matters for the base language model independent of the\ncross-document objective: final validation loss is \\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.graph}\nunder graph (bfs/dfs) packing versus \\val{traversal.wiki_val_loss.random} under random\npacking. The incremental cross-document benefit is itself traversal-robust --- the Java\ncross-document $\\Delta$nll above stays positive across all four bfs/dfs/random-walk/random\ntraversals --- so packing quality and cross-document attention are two decoupled axes.\n% The two validation-loss figures are read from training logs, not the eval harness, and\n% are tracked as grounding debt until a log-value source lands; see provenance/README.md.\n\n\\subsection{Diversity scaling (merged multi-source model)}\nAn 11-source merged model's cross-document $\\Delta$ is \\fillin{ratio $\\times$}\n\\fillin{larger/smaller?} than single-domain specialists (flat scaling\n\\fillin{params} $\\to$ \\fillin{params}), sug